#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120263 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119964 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "618f0ac5-e21a-5916-85f1-fbdeee648119" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Equation Governing the Current in an RL Circuit: Sinusoidal EMF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a9d91d0e-8b5a-5632-a09f-535db58568d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117723} {:answer/id #uuid "cc646d14-0593-5dec-b288-3369a0fa5bf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117724} {:answer/id #uuid "f1d4f391-fa26-5de9-ab15-9317b19d9ab6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117725} {:answer/id #uuid "02ac43f8-5308-5230-b8ef-8bacf1094585" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117726} {:answer/id #uuid "794cc58a-b1a0-54c6-a20d-36ee52d2e2dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117727}] :answer-field/id #uuid "b02dc0f6-e80f-5cb8-badb-b415dfadbdf3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117722}] :question/id #uuid "d82ca13b-67a8-5070-a065-c95927f9b6d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-336463" :question/problem "![](images/ea/ea1eb590e18a85672f176188f1bab96d85eb1be92d43edd322d1d032500a97ac.png)\n\nConsider the RL circuit shown above. Given that the current $I(t)$ at time $t$ seconds is given by\n$I(t) = Ae^{-14t} + α\\cos (2t) + β\\sin (2t)$,\nwhere $A$, $α$, and $β$ are constants, find the value of $α + β$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311728 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119963 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "88990b12-e7e0-5e21-9000-45357c07d04d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Equation Governing the Current in an RL Circuit: Constant EMF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b6d9eb43-6151-57e3-b17e-712357ab932f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310228}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b6d9eb43-6151-57e3-b17e-712357ab932f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310228} :answer-field/id #uuid "43600dac-6d25-5682-8937-5005846b9935" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310974}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "92ff7b67-920f-5e25-a5d9-2aca35a6388f" :question/math-academy-id "q-336643" :question/problem "The current $I(t),$ in amperes, in a series RL circuit is governed by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+6I=3,\\,I(0)=1.\n$$\n\nFind the steady-state current for this circuit.\n\nThe steady-state current is\n{{field-1}} amperes." :question/worked-solution "The equation is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+6{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-6t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${I}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0,$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+6\\alpha =3\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-6t}+\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}=A{e}^{-6t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The constant ${I}_{p}=\\frac{1}{2}$ is the *steady-state current*. As $t\\to \\infty ,$ the current $I$ approaches the value of the steady-state current.\n\nTherefore, the steady-state current is $\\frac{1}{2}$ amperes."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377225 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119963 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "88990b12-e7e0-5e21-9000-45357c07d04d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Equation Governing the Current in an RL Circuit: Constant EMF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b2deb0dc-4abd-518c-854a-8134786e852f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.85" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377227}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b2deb0dc-4abd-518c-854a-8134786e852f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.85" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377227} :answer-field/id #uuid "2791feb8-6710-5d48-88a6-a2f36341287f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377226}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5c397fb9-bf38-5a44-8a25-4f5a4f9ab367" :question/math-academy-id "q-336652" :question/problem "A series RL circuit has a source electromotive force (EMF) of $6\\,\\text{V},$ a resistance of $30\\,Ω,$ and an inductance of $3\\,\\text{H}.$ Find, **rounded to two decimal places**, the circuit's current after $0.02$ seconds given that the initial current is $1\\,\\text{A}.$\n\nThe current after $0.02$ seconds is\n{{field-1}} amperes." :question/worked-solution "Kirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=30\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=30I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=3\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=3\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n6=30I+3\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+10I=2.\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+10{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-10t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${I}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0,$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+10\\alpha =2\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =\\frac{1}{5}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{1}{5}.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-10t}+\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial current of $1\\,\\text{A}.$ So, we can apply the initial condition $I(0)=1,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(0) & =1 \\\\ A{e}^{-10(0)}+\\frac{1}{5} & =1 \\\\ A & =\\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $I(t):$\n\n$$\nI(t)=\\frac{4}{5}{e}^{-10t}+\\frac{1}{5}=\\frac{1}{5}(4{e}^{-10t}+1)\n$$\n\nFinally, the current in the circuit after $t=0.02$ seconds is\n\n$$\nI(0.02)=\\frac{1}{5}(4{e}^{-10(0.02)}+1)≈0.85\\,\\text{A},\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311743 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119964 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "618f0ac5-e21a-5916-85f1-fbdeee648119" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Equation Governing the Current in an RL Circuit: Sinusoidal EMF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0611168e-01b6-5eb5-9bdd-eb0c4ed87d21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{44}{169}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308796} {:answer/id #uuid "6b10d57c-e7e9-5a1a-9212-88743ad9bdcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{40}{169}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309579} {:answer/id #uuid "76c13a1a-1f33-531d-aa9c-39874842b71f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{38}{169}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309676} {:answer/id #uuid "c40b2d25-a48d-5c41-8009-e4d5ff3d8337" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{45}{169}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310320} {:answer/id #uuid "da9e72d4-e5ec-5e8d-a454-39ca5f817a72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{42}{169}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310503}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da9e72d4-e5ec-5e8d-a454-39ca5f817a72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{42}{169}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310503} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ea618d5-3a70-51f3-bce5-1d4b0166d37f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311085}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "195c82a8-9f40-5bf0-8acd-c9d6e6363995" :question/math-academy-id "q-336741" :question/problem "![](images/00/00d6676b631bd49c0c9b8a82f8dd05de3be6842f18b0c1c6b70f208e1d290d5a.png)\n\nConsider the RL circuit shown above. Given that the current $I(t)$ at time $t$ seconds is given by\n\n$$\nI(t)=A{e}^{-12t}+\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t),\n$$\n\nwhere $A,$ $\\alpha ,$ and $\\beta $ are constants, find the value of $\\alpha +\\beta .$" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $6\\,Ω,$ and an inductor of inductance $\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=6\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=6I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)=6I+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+12I=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+12{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-12t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $5t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $5t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t), \\\\ \\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt} & =-5\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)+5\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-5\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)+5\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t))+12(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)) & =6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ (-5\\alpha +12\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)+(12\\alpha +5\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t) & =6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-5\\alpha +12\\beta =6\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)) \\\\ 12\\alpha +5\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{30}{169},$ $\\beta =\\frac{72}{169}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{30}{169}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\frac{72}{169}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-12t}-\\frac{30}{169}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\frac{72}{169}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha =-\\frac{30}{169},\\,\\beta =\\frac{72}{169}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha +\\beta  & =-\\frac{30}{169}+\\frac{72}{169} \\\\  & =\\frac{42}{169}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
